Derive the following Two-Level Systems and the Schottky Anomaly relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
C_{V,1}=\frac{du}{dT}
=\frac{du}{dx}\frac{dx}{dT}
=\left(-\frac{\Delta e^x}{(1+e^x)^2}\right)\left(-\frac{x}{T}\right)
=k_{\mathrm B}x^2\frac{e^x}{(1+e^x)^2}
=k_{\mathrm B}\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^2\operatorname{sech}^2\left(\frac{x}{2}\right).
\tag*{[3]}
\]
Notation: \(0,\Delta\) denotes nondegenerate lower and upper one-particle energies; unit \(\mathrm{J, J}\); \(T,k_{\mathrm B},\beta\) denotes temperature, Boltzmann constant, and inverse thermal energy; unit \(\mathrm{K, J K^{-1}, J^{-1}}\); \(x\) denotes dimensionless gap, beta Delta; dimensionless; \(z_1\) denotes one-particle partition function; dimensionless; \(u,C_{V,1},C_{V,1,\mathrm{max}}\) denotes one-particle mean energy, constant-volume heat capacity, and its maximum; unit \(\mathrm{J, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(p_1\) denotes upper-level probability; dimensionless.

